13915	{"artifactId":"LLD-b7a68147dcf72034-s3","entry":{"id":"review-8b755690c5967c7b4cf699ffc77f290e","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-23T11:18:55.143Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.story","stage":"design.story","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The 2026-10-23 decisions are all designed. A global flag (manual/edit-auto/auto) defaults to auto. A chat's edits mode overrides it for that call. A common call asks in manual and edit-auto. A destructive call always asks. The eight destructive:true builtins map to destructive. buildApprovalGate is related to the request.","evidence":"docs/plans/tool-registry.md:114-120 says: 'global approval flag ... same three values ... When a call comes from a chat session, that session's edits mode overrides it ... Default for the flag: auto' and 'maps the 8 builtins that set destructive: true ... states how the existing buildApprovalGate relates'. The LLD covers these in sections 2.2, 2.4, 3.2, 3.5 and the section 5 edge cases. Its ac6 reading matches the HLD's deferred question q6953cb69 (HLD.md:309). [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.2","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The s3 design run carries what the epic plan requires of it, including filing the sc2/sc3 HLD amendments.","evidence":"docs/plans/tool-registry.md:137 says: 'The HLD amendments for these two contracts are filed in the s3 design run ... and are approved before the s4 LLD is written.' LLD section 10 admits the sc2 and sc3 amendments 'were not filed in this run's s4 and s5 steps'. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md]","action":"File the sc2 amendment (typebox TSchema, TypedTool, callTool signature) and the sc3 amendment (convertText(req, deps), keyValues required) before the s4 LLD starts, or record the user's decision to move that obligation elsewhere.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"executeTool treats requiresApproval (true or a predicate) as approved, logs 'auto-approving' and runs the tool. Tool carries requiresApproval and destructive. ToolDeps has no caller or approval-mode field.","evidence":"executor.ts:41-45: 'const needsGate = tool.requiresApproval === true || (typeof tool.requiresApproval === 'function' && ...) ... log.warn(... auto-approving)', then execute runs. types.ts:118 has requiresApproval, types.ts:128 has destructive, and ToolDeps (types.ts:50-81) has neither caller nor approvalMode. [files: src/daemon/tools/executor.ts, src/daemon/tools/types.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The IPC server pushes only over a client-opened stream and aborts the handler's signal when the socket closes. IpcStreamKind already lists 'gate'. gate.request-permission is an offline stub.","evidence":"server.ts:261-267: 'const abortController = new AbortController(); const onClose = () => { abortController.abort(); }; socket.on('close', onClose)'. shared/types.ts:905 lists 'gate' in IpcStreamKind. index.ts:1931: \"'gate.request-permission': offlineStream('gate.request-permission')\". todos.subscribe (index.ts:1937) is a live long-running stream to model the subscribe handler on. [files: src/daemon/server.ts, src/shared/types.ts, src/daemon/index.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.3","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"approval.defaultAction and destructive.requireDoubleConfirm are read by nothing outside src/daemon/tools/config.ts.","evidence":"A grep over every *.ts/*.tsx file finds the two keys only in config.ts (lines 30, 50, 74, 79, 109, 129, 147). Line 147 is a log field. No tool or client reads them. [files: src/daemon/tools/config.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Eight builtins set destructive:true. Existing requiresApproval declarations are true, a predicate, or false: true maps to common, a predicate to a per-input function, false to none. The 77 false declarations sit in 55 files, including src/docgen/tool.ts.","evidence":"The grep for 'destructive:\\s*true' finds exactly 8: az/vm.ts:238, aws/s3.ts:210, aws/cloudformation.ts:235, aws/ec2.ts:255, gcp/storage.ts:189, gcp/compute.ts:247, gcp/run.ts:212, k8s/index.ts:340. 'requiresApproval: false' gives 77 hits in 55 files, one of them src/docgen/tool.ts:56. Every requiresApproval declaration in a builtin is a boolean literal, so the predicate mapping has no current user. It covers only the type's predicate form. [files: src/daemon/tools/types.ts, src/docgen/tool.ts]","action":"Note in the plan that no builtin uses a predicate today, so the per-input mapping is only exercised by test fixtures.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The client streaming path is new: IpcClient has no streaming method, and the terminal client has no stream subscription today.","evidence":"shared/ipc-client.ts:121-138: the IpcClient interface has only rpc, status, reachability, update and updateOutcome. src/cli has no stream:true or subscribe call (the only hit is a comment in LogsSection.tsx). However, the plugin already has a chat approval queue, vscode-plugin/src/chat/approval-queue.ts ('permission requests are asked one at a time'), with an approval card. The LLD does not say whether the daemon's gate frames reuse it. [files: src/shared/ipc-client.ts, vscode-plugin/src/chat/approval-queue.ts]","action":"In the plan, state that the chat client feeds approval.subscribe gate frames into the existing approval-queue.ts and card rather than building a second prompt path.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Of the ToolDeps builders, only src/analyze/context/driver.ts and src/agent/providers/ollama.ts reach executeTool. code-review/grounding.ts and build/changed-files.ts call gitDiffTool.execute directly.","evidence":"driver.ts:1114-1133 (buildToolDeps) and ollama.ts:686 pass deps into runReadOnlyToolLoop, which calls executeTool (read-only-tool-loop.ts:78). grounding.ts:370-371 and changed-files.ts:110-111 call 'gitDiffTool.execute({ cwd: repoPath, ...input }, toolDeps)'. Nothing else calls executeTool. Note: the shared caller is src/agent/read-only-tool-loop.ts, which spreads args.deps (line 80), so setting caller at the two builders reaches the executor. [files: src/analyze/context/driver.ts, src/agent/providers/ollama.ts, src/agent/read-only-tool-loop.ts, src/workflow/code-review/grounding.ts, src/workflow/runners/build/changed-files.ts]","action":"Optionally list src/agent/read-only-tool-loop.ts as the executeTool call site.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"change-sites.2","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"The call-site list for the new streaming method on IpcClient is complete: src/shared/ipc-client.ts, src/cli/services and the vscode-plugin chat, daemon and extension modules.","evidence":"Test fakes type themselves as the full interface: vscode-plugin/src/config/__tests__/per-role-engine.test.ts:145 'const fakeClient: IpcClient = {' and vscode-plugin/src/workspace/__tests__/registrar.test.ts:16 'const client: IpcClient = {'. Adding a required method fails typecheck in both. activation.test.ts and controller.test.ts also import IpcClient. None of these is listed. [files: src/shared/ipc-client.ts, vscode-plugin/src/config/__tests__/per-role-engine.test.ts, vscode-plugin/src/workspace/__tests__/registrar.test.ts]","action":"Add the IpcClient test doubles (per-role-engine.test.ts, registrar.test.ts, and any others that build an IpcClient literal) to the change sites, or make the new method optional.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"approval.commonMode can live in the ToolSettings snapshot, set by the existing tools.config.set RPC, and act as the daemon's global flag.","evidence":"config.ts:4-5: 'The IDE pushes its insrc.tools.* settings to the daemon on connect ... via the tools.config.set RPC'. That IDE is deprecated, and a repo-wide grep finds 'tools.config' only in src/daemon/index.ts:1708 (the handler) and config.ts; no current client sends it. The snapshot is in memory (config.ts:93 'let current: ToolSettings = defaults()'). So the flag is not persisted, resets to auto on every daemon restart, and nothing can set it. tool-registry.md:116 asks for 'a global approval flag in the daemon's config'. [files: src/daemon/tools/config.ts, src/daemon/index.ts, docs/plans/tool-registry.md]","action":"Persist approval.commonMode in the daemon's on-disk config (src/config store) and read it at startup, or name the client that sets it and design how it persists. Add a test that the value survives a restart.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.2","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Removing approval.defaultAction and destructive.requireDoubleConfirm breaks no stored or sent record.","evidence":"updateToolSettings (config.ts:106-150) reads only the keys it names, so an old payload carrying the removed keys is ignored. No client sends them (grep over *.ts and *.tsx), and nothing persists ToolSettings. [files: src/daemon/tools/config.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"s3 reuses s2's ToolDenialReason for the denied ToolFailure, including the reason 'cancelled', without changing s2's contract.","evidence":"S002/LLD.md:153 says: 'ToolDenialReason is 'declined' | 'timed-out' | 'no-client', declared here so that this Story does not depend on s3; s3 reuses it.' The HLD sketch (HLD.md:114) also has ApprovalDenial = 'declined' | 'timed-out' | 'no-client'. The s3 LLD adds 'cancelled' (section 3.3 and the section 5 abort path: 'executor returns a denied failure with reason cancelled'). s2 owns sc5 and ToolFailure.denial, so the type it owns does not carry s3's value. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/S002/LLD.md, docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/HLD.md]","action":"Raise an sc5 amendment adding 'cancelled' to ToolDenialReason, or have s3 map an abort to an existing reason or failure kind. Say which in the LLD.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"does-not-hold"},{"claimId":"boundaries.2","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"s3 does not build s7's tool-call IPC method. It defines only the caller and approvalMode fields that s7 must carry, under sc4, which s3 owns.","evidence":"tool-registry.md:105 says: 'The IPC method's shape and its caller value ... are s3 and s7 design.' LLD section 4 keeps the method in s7 and defines the fields as part of sc4. [files: docs/plans/tool-registry.md]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"When every subscriber disconnects, it can be detected through the subscribe handler's abort signal, so pending requests can resolve no-client at once.","evidence":"server.ts:264-267 aborts the stream handler's signal on socket 'close' and 'error', and sendStream aborts on a write failure (lines 271-278). A subscribe handler that holds its signal can therefore drop its stream from the broker's set. The per-request timer, abort via deps.signal (types.ts:60) and the unknown-requestId lookup are each detectable as stated. [files: src/daemon/server.ts, src/daemon/tools/types.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each of ac1 to ac7 maps to at least one named test, at a location the test sweep runs.","evidence":"Section 6's acceptance mapping covers ac1 to ac7 (DEF.md:138-144). Each criterion names approval-broker, executor-approval, approval-class-mapping or approval-ipc tests under src/daemon/tools/__tests__, a directory the sweep glob 'src/**/__tests__/*.test.ts' covers (it does not exist yet). The ac6 and ac7 client tests go under src/cli/services/__tests__ and vscode-plugin/src/chat/__tests__, where approval-queue.test.ts already lives. The vscode-plugin tests are outside the root sweep glob. [files: docs/epics/one-tool-registry-git-gh-jira-E20261023b7a68147/DEF.md]","action":"The plan should name the command that runs the vscode-plugin test so the build gate can run it.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":4,"low":11,"unverified":0},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-23T11:18:55.143Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
